import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { SplitterHero } from "../../demos/splitter/hero.rx"
import { SplitterNested } from "../../demos/splitter/nested.rx"
import { SplitterCollapsible } from "../../demos/splitter/collapsible.rx"
import { SplitterControlled } from "../../demos/splitter/controlled.rx"
import { SplitterSaving } from "../../demos/splitter/saving.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Splitter" subtitle="Panels separated by bars that resize them, with a pointer or the keyboard.">
    <Demo source="splitter/hero"><SplitterHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together, a handle between each pair of panels:</p>
    <Code file="splitter/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing that makes a splitter. CSS <code>resize</code> is supported everywhere, but it resizes one element
      from a grip in its corner: the grip isn't a separator, can't be reached with <kbd>Tab</kbd> and doesn't
      answer the arrow keys (the family's tests check this in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit), and it can't share
      space between neighbours or keep them within bounds. So the library does it, following the WAI-ARIA
      Authoring Practices' Window Splitter pattern: each handle is a focusable <code>role="separator"</code>{" "}
      whose value is the size of the panel before it, with <code>aria-controls</code> naming that panel.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the library adds is the layout and the input: the sizes (percentages that always sum to 100, within
      each panel's bounds), pointer dragging with pointer capture, and the keys. The arrow keys along the group's
      axis move a handle by <code>keyboardStep</code>, mirrored in a right-to-left layout; <kbd>Home</kbd> and{" "}
      <kbd>End</kbd> give the panel before it the smallest and largest size it can reach, exactly the minimum and
      maximum the handle announces (for a collapsible panel the smallest is its collapsed size, so{" "}
      <kbd>Home</kbd> collapses it, as the pattern allows); <kbd>Enter</kbd> collapses a
      collapsible panel and restores its previous size. A double-click on a handle puts the default sizes back.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sizes reach the page as CSS you can see in the inspector, written inline because sizing can't work
      without it: the root is <code>display: flex</code> in the orientation's direction, and each panel is{" "}
      <code>flex: &lt;size&gt; 1 0px</code> with <code>min-width: 0</code> (<code>min-height</code> when
      stacked). A grow factor on a zero basis makes each size a percentage of the space left after the handles,
      however thick you make them, where a percentage width or a <code>--panel-size</code> variable would need
      the handles subtracted. <code>min-width: 0</code> lets a panel shrink below its content, which a flex item
      otherwise refuses. Handles get <code>touch-action: none</code>, so a touch drag moves them instead of
      scrolling. Everything else, including the handle's size, the cursor and <code>overflow</code>, is your CSS.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        A handle is a focusable control, so it needs a name: give each one an <code>aria-label</code> (or{" "}
        <code>aria-labelledby</code>) that says what it resizes, such as "Resize files".
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="sizes">Sizes and bounds</h3>
    <p>
      Sizes are percentages. Give a panel a <code>defaultSize</code>, or leave it out to share what the others
      leave; or pass the whole layout as <code>defaultSizes</code> on the root, one size per panel in document
      order. <code>minSize</code> and <code>maxSize</code> bound a panel. Dragging a handle takes space from the
      nearest panels on the side it moves towards that can still shrink, one after the other, and gives it to the
      panel beside the handle on the other side, then to the next once that one reaches its maximum. Each frame of
      a drag is worked out from where the press began, so dragging past a bound and back puts every panel back.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each panel needs an <code>id</code>, unique in its group. A panel added or removed after the splitter has
      appeared keeps the others' sizes by their ids: a new one takes its default size and the rest share what
      remains in proportion; a removed one's space goes to the others in proportion.
    </p>

    <h3 id="nested">Stacked, and nested</h3>
    <p>
      <code>orientation="vertical"</code> stacks the panels; the arrow keys are then <kbd>Up</kbd> and{" "}
      <kbd>Down</kbd>. A splitter inside another's panel is independent: its handles, keys and drags are its own.
      The handle's <code>aria-orientation</code> is the bar's own (<code>vertical</code> between side-by-side
      panels), while every part's <code>data-orientation</code> is the group's, so one selector styles a whole
      group.
    </p>
    <Demo source="splitter/nested"><SplitterNested /></Demo>

    <h3 id="collapsible">Collapsing a panel</h3>
    <p>
      A <code>collapsible</code> panel collapses to its <code>collapsedSize</code> when dragged past halfway
      between that and its minimum, when an arrow key would take it below its minimum, on <kbd>Home</kbd> on the
      handle after it, or on <kbd>Enter</kbd> on the handle beside it; <kbd>Enter</kbd> again restores the size it
      had. The handle's <code>aria-valuemin</code> is then the collapsed size, which is where <kbd>Home</kbd> goes;{" "}
      <kbd>End</kbd> goes to its <code>aria-valuemax</code>: the panel's <code>maxSize</code>, or less if the
      panels after the handle can't shrink that far. A panel that starts collapsed doesn't call{" "}
      <code>onCollapse</code>. It never stops in between. It carries{" "}
      <code>data-collapsed</code> while collapsed, and calls <code>onCollapse</code> and <code>onExpand</code>.{" "}
      <kbd>Enter</kbd> acts on the panel before the handle, or on the one after it when only that one is
      collapsible, as a sidebar on the far side is.
    </p>
    <Demo source="splitter/collapsible"><SplitterCollapsible /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>sizes</code> and <code>onSizesChange</code> to hold the layout yourself.{" "}
      <code>onSizesChange</code> is called on every change, many times during a drag; <code>onSizesCommit</code>{" "}
      when a change is final: on release, on each key and on a reset. Both get the sizes and a{" "}
      <code>details</code> object whose <code>reason</code> is <code>drag</code>, <code>keyboard</code> or{" "}
      <code>reset</code>. Sizes you pass that don't sum to 100 or break a panel's bounds are corrected; a list
      without one size per panel is ignored in favour of the defaults.
    </p>
    <Demo source="splitter/controlled"><SplitterControlled /></Demo>

    <h3 id="saving">Saving the layout</h3>
    <p>
      To keep a layout, save it in <code>onSizesCommit</code>, to a server or to storage: the splitter has no
      storage option of its own. The handler is called in a change of its own, so it can be an action that waits
      for a server, and the panels can be dragged and stepped again while the save is out. Read a saved layout
      back as <code>defaultSizes</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo source="splitter/saving"><SplitterSaving /></Demo>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling the drag</h3>
    <p>
      The root and the handle being dragged carry <code>data-dragging</code>. Set the cursor and{" "}
      <code>user-select: none</code> on the root there, so they hold even when the pointer leaves the handle.
      Pressing a handle focuses it; style focus with <code>:focus-visible</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="splitter" />
  </DocsPage>
}
